A||||9||Scott2M4||Sound of flowing fluid||||5||
Z||000000||Scott2M4||12-29-1999||10:42 AM||||This week has seen temperatures drop into the teens at night.  Over the last few mornings, when activating the <B>defroster</B>, I hear a sound like fluid flowing through pipes.<P>I want to ask if this is normal but it can't be!  I've had the car for a few months now and have never heard this sound before.  Is this simply a result of the cold weather?<P>BTW, I checked and I don't see any coolant dripping onto the passenger floor mat (I did a search before posting and got several <B>heater core</B> threads).||||reg||5
Z||000001||Raydar||12-29-1999||11:07 AM||||Scott, Have you checked your antifreeze? Your coolant might be turning to slush, if it's "right on the edge". Seems like you'd be able to hear it flowing through the heater core, when you first start up. <BR>SWAG. (Scientific Wild-A$$ Guess.)||||reg||1
Z||000002||batboy||12-29-1999||12:04 PM||||You may have a little air in the system or the coolant is just a little low. Check the coolant level and burp it if needed.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Monkeyman||12-29-1999||12:56 PM||||How would you go about "burping" a car?  Put it on your shoulder and pat it's back? <P> Beelllch!  Oh, sorry!  I feel better now.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Rodrv6||12-29-1999||05:09 PM||||Scott, I agree with Raydar.  On a couple of occasions when the temps get down into the teens I have heard the same type of noise.  Both times I had problems with the coolant freezing in the lines just enough to make the engine run too hot.  Fresh anti-freeze in the proper concentration solved the problem.||||reg||4
Z||000005||Raydar||12-29-1999||10:31 PM||||Yeah. What Batboy said. Air could do it, too. Forgot about that. ||||reg||1
Z||000006||dogtired||12-30-1999||06:54 AM||||STOP,  go directly to your car, DO NOT pass go DO NOT collect $200. Do it NOW. If your coolant is so weak it is making slush, you need to get that taken care of NOW!!!  OK so maybe blowing the freeze plugs out of your motor is a good reason to do a rebuild, but I think NOT.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>If I got a second guess, it would be air in the system???   Good LUCK.||||reg||6
Z||000007||Monkeyman||12-30-1999||08:57 AM||||I had some air in my system the other day.  Tried to blame it on my wife.  She wasn't happy 'cause I tried to blame it on her in the middle of WalMart.     hehehehehehehe||||reg||1
Z||000008||overlord||12-30-1999||09:44 AM||||i live in canada..(sub sub zero temps)<P>and that sound you hear is normal as far as im concerneed.<P>my anitfreeze is topnotch and alwsays full,and it always does that when i first start er up.||||reg||1
Z||000009||fiero56||12-30-1999||07:12 PM||||My formula makes that noise on occasion too Scott. Its been a few days since I heard it, but I recall it seemed to be coming from the passenger side footwell. I may have air in the system since I still haven't gotten a new coolannt line yet or it may just be something Fieros do. You can get a little coolant checker. I have one that has little balls in it that float. It says my coolant is good well below freezing,plus the coolant is only a few months old, so I don't think it is turning to slush. If you need to purge the system of air, it's really not that hard or complicated. ||||reg||1
